course: dictionary
lesson: 3965
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word fuse is a good example. «Each hip bone looks like one bone but is really three fused together» uses fuse as a verb. The bones are joined into one, so fused means joined or melted together.
explain: «Homes are full of guards: shutters inside sockets, fuses that melt, and switches in the fuse box» uses fuse as a noun. Here it is a small safety part in electricity. The words melt and guards show that its job is to protect you, so you know it is not a firework.
explain: Phrases can change the meaning completely. A person with a short fuse gets angry quickly, and nobody is talking about wires at all. When you meet fuse, ask: what is the sentence about? Electricity, bones, a bang, or a person's feelings? That question tells you which meaning to use.
fact: A fuse is a small safety part in an electric plug or circuit. It has a thin wire that melts and breaks the circuit if too much current flows.
fact: A fuse can also be a string or cord that you light to set off a firework or explosive, as in «the fuse burns down before a bang».
fact: As a verb, to fuse means to join or melt together into one.
fact: In the UK, if a light or machine fuses, it stops working because its fuse has melted.
fact: Never replace a fuse with wire or foil. Fuses are there to protect you from fire.
exercise: In «fuses that melt», what is a fuse? | *A small safety part that melts if too much current flows | Two things joined into one | A string you light to set off a firework | A type of electric plug
exercise: «Each hip bone looks like one bone but is really three fused together.» What does fused mean? | Broken into small pieces | Lit up with a bang | Put away in a box | *Joined together into one
exercise: Sam says, «She has a short fuse.» What does it mean? | She is very tall | She has a very thin wire | She is good at setting off fireworks | *She gets angry quickly
test: Why can too much current start a fire? | Because the fuse box makes the room brighter | *Because it makes wires hot enough to start a fire | Because fire likes electric plugs | Because wires become thinner and lighter
test: In a story, «the fuse burned down before the bang», what is the fuse? | A box that holds the lights | Two bones joined together | *A cord that burns to set off an explosion | A wire inside a plug
exam: Which sentence uses fuse as a verb? | The fuse box in the hall has switches for each circuit | *The hip bones are fused together, so they act as one bone | Never replace a fuse with wire or foil | Light the fuse and run back to safety
